Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
#include <iostream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::string line;
    std::istringstream is(markdown);

    while (std::getline(is, line)) {
        html += line + "\n";
    }

    return html;
}
```

This function takes a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text as input and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. It uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line and appends each line to the `html` string, with a newline character separating each line.

Here's an example usage of the function:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello, world!\nThis is a paragraph.\nThis is a list item:\n- First item\n- Second item";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output the HTML formatted text, which includes a heading, a paragraph, and a list item with two items.

